==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈfɛk.ʃəs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Ajshul-infectious.wav|Audio (Canada)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|pathology|of an illness}} Caused by an agent that enters the host's body (such as a [[bacterium]], [[virus]], [[parasite]], or [[prion]]); often, also, [[transmit]]ted among hosts via any of various routes (for example, contact, droplet-borne, airborne, waterborne, foodborne, [[fomite]]-borne, or bloodborne).
#: {{ux|en|Cancer is usually not '''infectious'''.}}<!--HPV-induced cervical cancer in humans and virally induced cancer in other animals are exceptions, but most cancers are not infectious.-->
#: {{ux|en|More '''infectious''' diseases like the flu are usually less potent.}}
# {{lb|en|pathology|typically|of a person}} Able to [[infect]] others; capable of transmitting pathogens.
#: {{ux|en|Despite feeling better, the patient is still '''infectious'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=01 Dec 81|volume=12|number=20|journal=Gay Community News|page=3|author=Sue Hyde|title=AIDS Update: Little To Cheer About|quotee=Paul Black|text=I doubt that [saliva] is very '''infectious''' ... I don't think this is a normal route of transmission.}}
# {{lb|en|of feelings and behaviour}} Spreading quickly from one person to another.
#: {{ux|en|Her enthusiasm for work can be really '''infectious'''.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} [[memorable|Memorable]] and invoking [[excitement]] or [[interest]].
#: {{ux|en|Pop music is more '''infectious''' than elevator music.}}

====Usage notes====
The terms {{m|en|infectious}}, {{m|en|communicable}}, {{m|en|transmissible}}/{{m|en|transmittable}}, and {{m|en|contagious}}, as well as {{m|en|spreadable}} and {{m|en|catching}}, overlap on a [[semantic field]] and are often loosely used synonymously in their broad senses, although they are differentiable by narrower senses, as follows:
* The word {{m|en|infectious}} describes any disease or condition that is caused by an infectious agent (such as a [[bacterium]], [[virus]], [[parasite]], or [[prion]]), including ones ''with'' person-to-person transmission/spread and ones ''without'' person-to-person transmission/spread. It is thus [[hypernym]]ous to the following terms.
* The words {{m|en|communicable}} and {{m|en|transmissible}}/{{m|en|transmittable}}, as well as {{m|en|spreadable}} and {{m|en|catching}} (which are informal), describe the large subset of infectious diseases in which person-to-person transmission/spread (communication) can occur, including ones that are ''readily/easily'' spread and ones that are ''not'' readily/easily spread. They are thus [[hypernym]]ous to the following term.
* The word {{m|en|contagious}} describes only those infectious diseases that are readily/easily spread, to the degree that preventing their spread is quite difficult unless a population is highly vaccinated against them (examples include measles and diphtheria).

